    $astroDisk_master$> This is the causal heart of the block. Everything before it was preparation — the yardstick, the Sun and its flux gradient, the collapse that made a rotating disk — and everything after is consequence. Here we examine the **protoplanetary disk** and establish the single fact from which the architecture of the entire solar system follows: **the disk had a temperature gradient, and that gradient decided which materials could freeze into solid grains at which distances.** Near the young Sun only metal and rock could solidify; past a boundary around **2.7 AU** it was cold enough for water to freeze into ice — and because water is far more abundant than rock, crossing that line multiplied the available solid material two- to threefold. That multiplication was decisive, because planet formation was a *race* against the disappearing gas. Outside the line the race was winnable and giants formed; inside it was not, and only rock was left. **Four small rocky worlds and four giants: one boundary, one consequence.** We will derive the line's position (and get a satisfying 2.71 AU), walk the full condensation sequence, and — most convincingly — read the fossils the snow line left behind, which are still there to see.

## The boundary you can still see

Go out to the asteroid belt, between Mars and Jupiter, from about 2.1 to 3.3 AU. Something about it is easy to miss and, once seen, startling: **the asteroids are not all the same, and their composition changes systematically with distance from the Sun.**

In the *inner* belt, from roughly 2.0 to 2.5 AU, the dominant bodies are **S-type** asteroids — stony, made of silicate rock and metal, and *dry.* The best-studied example, Vesta at 2.36 AU, is a differentiated little world with a basaltic crust, an iron core, and essentially no water. In the *outer* belt, from about 2.7 to 3.5 AU, the population shifts to **C-type** asteroids — dark, carbonaceous, and rich in carbon compounds and, crucially, **water**, bound in hydrated minerals and, in the larger bodies, present as ice. The largest object in the belt, **Ceres**, at 2.77 AU, is estimated to be something like **a quarter water by mass** — more like an icy moon than a rock.

{{image: Ceres (dwarf planet) | Ceres, the largest body in the asteroid belt, photographed by the Dawn spacecraft. At 2.77 AU — just outside the snow line — it is estimated to be roughly a quarter water by mass, an object more like an icy moon than a rock. Its dry, stony neighbour Vesta, at 2.36 AU on the other side of the line, has essentially no water at all. Two bodies in the same belt, less than half an astronomical unit apart, one bone-dry and one a quarter ice: the snow line, preserved in place for four and a half billion years.}}

So: dry stony bodies on one side, wet carbonaceous bodies on the other, with the transition around 2.7 AU. Now ask why. These bodies are *leftovers* — planetesimals that never assembled into a planet, and so have sat roughly where the disk made them for four and a half billion years. They are samples of the disk, and the composition they record is the composition of the disk **at the place and moment they formed.** The inner ones are dry because they condensed where it was too warm for water ice; the outer ones are wet because they condensed where it was cold enough for water to freeze. The transition marks where, long ago, the temperature of the solar nebula crossed the freezing point of water. **That boundary is the snow line, and it is still there — you can point at it.** The same boundary that sorted the asteroids into wet and dry also sorted the *planets* into rocky and giant. This chapter is about that line: where it came from, why it sits where it does, and how one temperature boundary in a vanished disk of gas determined the architecture of everything we will study.

## The disk: how much, how big, how arranged

Before temperatures, a working picture of the disk. We cannot observe it — it vanished four and a half billion years ago — but we can reconstruct a *lower bound* on its mass by a clever trick, giving the **minimum-mass solar nebula**. Take each planet's inventory of heavy elements, and add back enough hydrogen and helium to restore solar composition (planets are heavily depleted in those gases). That gives the disk material each planet needed; spread over the disk and summed, it yields a total. The answer is roughly **0.01–0.02 solar masses**, about **4,300 Earth masses** of gas and dust. It is called a *minimum* for good reason — it assumes every scrap of solid was efficiently used, which is false, so the real nebula was likely several times heavier. And the number is striking against the **447 Earth masses** the planets actually total today: **the disk held at least ten to thirty times more material than survives in the planets**, the overwhelming majority of it hydrogen and helium gas that was blown away when the disk dispersed. The solar system is the residue of a residue.

The reconstruction also gives the disk's shape. Surface density fell steeply with distance, roughly as $\Sigma(r) \propto r^{-3/2}$ — material strongly concentrated toward the centre, thinning fast outward. That matters: far out there was simply less stuff per unit area, and orbital periods were long, so growth was slow — part of why Uranus and Neptune stayed smaller than Jupiter and Saturn despite also forming beyond the snow line.

```example
surface-density
```

Finally, composition. The disk inherited the composition of its parent cloud, which is essentially the composition of the universe: about **98.5% hydrogen and helium gas**, and roughly **1% solids** — within which water was 0.6%, rock and metal 0.4%, and other volatiles the rest. Everything solid in the solar system — every planet, moon, asteroid, and the ground beneath you — was built from that 1%. But look closely at the breakdown, because the next three sections turn on it: **water alone (0.6%) outweighs all rock and metal combined (0.4%).** That single line is the reason the snow line matters. Hold onto it.

## The condensation sequence: what freezes where

Now the chemistry. A disk of hot gas cooling down does not condense all at once; different materials solidify at different temperatures, in a definite order — the **condensation sequence**, the mechanism that translates a temperature gradient into a compositional one. As gas of cosmic composition cools, refractory oxides freeze out first (below ~1,700 K), then iron–nickel metal (~1,400 K), then silicate rock (~1,300 K), then — far cooler — **water ice at about 170 K**, then ammonia (~100 K), methane (~40 K), and carbon monoxide and nitrogen ices last of all (~25 K). The pattern is intuitive: *refractory* materials, with strong bonds and high melting points, condense almost anywhere; *volatile* ices need progressively colder conditions and can only condense far out.

Here is the key move. Because the disk's temperature *falls with distance*, the condensation sequence in temperature translates directly into a sequence in **space**. Each material has a distance inside which it is vapour and outside which it is a solid grain — every condensation temperature defines a *line* in the disk. Drag through the disk and watch the inventory of solids grow as it cools:

```condensation
```

So the disk was chemically zoned: innermost, only refractory oxides and metals (and in the hottest regions, nothing at all); through the inner disk, metals and silicate rock — the terrestrial-planet zone; beyond ~170 K, water ice joins the inventory — the snow line; and farther still, the ammonia, methane, and nitrogen lines that set the stage for the ice giants and the frozen bodies of the Kuiper Belt. **The disk was not a uniform soup but a chemically stratified structure, and each planet inherited the composition of the zone where it formed.** That is the central idea; everything else is consequence. There are many condensation lines, one per species — but one matters far more than the rest, for a reason of pure abundance.

## Locating the line: a derivation

Let us actually compute where water ice condenses. The disk is heated mainly by the young Sun's light, which it absorbs and re-radiates; energy balance for such a disk gives a temperature profile falling as $T(r) \propto r^{-1/2}$. This follows straight from the inverse-square law: the flux falls as $r^{-2}$, and a body in radiative equilibrium has $T \propto F^{1/4}$, so $T \propto (r^{-2})^{1/4} = r^{-1/2}$. Normalizing at Earth's orbit, where equilibrium temperature is about 280 K, gives $T(r) \approx 280\,\text{K} \times r^{-1/2}$. Now set that equal to water ice's condensation point, 170 K, and solve:

$$170 = 280\,r^{-1/2} \quad\Rightarrow\quad r = \left(\frac{280}{170}\right)^2 = \mathbf{2.71\ AU}$$

**The simple calculation gives 2.71 AU** — squarely in the asteroid belt, essentially exactly where the observed transition between dry and wet asteroids occurs. I find this genuinely satisfying: a two-line calculation, using nothing but the inverse-square law and the freezing point of water, predicts the most consequential boundary in the solar system — and objects still sitting there confirm it.

```example
condensation-line
```

One honest complication: **the line moved.** The calculation treats the disk as passively heated by starlight, but young disks also generate heat internally, from friction as material spirals inward (the same viscosity that transports angular momentum). In its early, massive phase the disk was hotter than passive reprocessing alone, pushing the snow line *farther out* — perhaps beyond 5 AU. As accretion slowed and the disk cooled, the line **swept inward** over the disk's few-million-year lifetime. So the snow line is better pictured as a boundary that migrated inward through the disk, and the "2.7 AU" figure should be read as characteristic rather than exact. **The concept is robust; the precise number is model-dependent** — a distinction worth internalizing. The *existence* of a major condensation boundary is certain, directly evidenced by the belt's composition; its exact position and history are matters of ongoing modelling.

## Why one line changed everything: the solid density jump

We reach the causal core — the step that turns a chemistry boundary into a planetary architecture. Return to the composition and ask what is available as *solid* on either side. **Inside the snow line**, only rock and metal can condense — about **0.4%** of the nebula's mass. **Outside**, rock and metal condense *plus* water ice — about **1.0%**. The jump is $1.0/0.4 = \mathbf{2.5\times}$ (the literature quotes 2–4×; the precise value does not matter, only that the step is large).

```example
solid-jump
```

Crossing the snow line more than doubles the solid material available to build planets — and it matters *only because water is abundant.* Hydrogen is the most common element in the universe and oxygen the third, so water is one of the most common compounds there is, outweighing all rock and metal put together. Had the substance freezing at 170 K been something rare, crossing its line would have made no difference. **The architecture of the solar system rests on the cosmic abundance of hydrogen and oxygen.**

But why should *more material* produce a categorically different planet rather than merely a bigger one? Because of the **deadline**. The disk's gas dispersed in roughly 3–10 million years. And giant-planet formation works like this: a solid core grows by accretion, holding only a thin atmosphere while small; but once it reaches a critical mass — roughly **ten Earth masses** — its gravity triggers **runaway gas accretion**, pulling in hydrogen and helium far faster than it can shed the heat, and rapidly swallowing an enormous envelope to become a gas giant. This only works *while the gas is still there.* So planet formation is a race: **reach ten Earth masses of core before the gas disperses, and you become a giant; fail, and you stay a rock — however large you eventually grow.** Now the snow line's meaning is clear. Outside, with two to three times more solids, cores reached ten Earth masses inside the window; inside, with the sparse rock-and-metal inventory, they could not. Jupiter, forming just outside the line where solids were most abundant, won most decisively and grew largest. Earth, forming inside, never came close — it reached its final mass long after the gas was gone, and has no primordial hydrogen envelope at all, only the thin secondary atmosphere it later outgassed. We can now write the chain complete:

$$\text{distance} \rightarrow \text{temperature} \rightarrow \text{what condenses} \rightarrow \text{solid density} \rightarrow \text{growth rate} \rightarrow \text{core mass at gas dispersal} \rightarrow \text{rock or giant}$$

Every arrow is physics we have established. **Four small rocky worlds inside, four giants outside — because water is abundant and freezes at 170 K.** That is the architecture of the solar system, explained.

## The fossils: three independent confirmations

A theory that merely restates the fact it was built for is worth little. What makes the snow-line picture convincing is that it predicts *further* consequences, in independent data, which turn out to be there. Explore them:

```densitydivide
```

**The densities of the planets** are the most direct test. If the inner worlds were built from rock and metal alone while the outer ones added abundant ice and captured hydrogen and helium, the two groups should differ sharply in density — and they do, with no overlap: the inner four run 3,900–5,500 kg/m³ (rock and iron), the outer four 690–1,640 (gas and ice). And note the entry that always delights students: **Saturn's mean density is 687 kg/m³ — less than water.** A world 95 times Earth's mass would, given a large enough ocean, float. That is what it means to be built beyond the snow line from the lightest materials in the universe.

{{image: Saturn | Saturn, imaged by the Cassini spacecraft. Its mean density is just 687 kg/m³ — less than that of water — so a world ninety-five times Earth's mass would, given a large enough ocean, float. That absurd-sounding fact is a direct fossil of the snow line: Saturn formed beyond it, where abundant ice let a large core assemble fast enough to seize an enormous envelope of hydrogen and helium, the two lightest elements in the universe. No planet inside the snow line comes anywhere near this density.}}

**The asteroid belt gradient** — our cold open — is more impressive still, because it is *positional*: composition varies with distance across a single population, at the predicted location. Dry S-type asteroids (Vesta, 2.36 AU) give way to wet C-type ones (Ceres, 2.77 AU, a quarter water) right around the snow line. Two bodies in the same belt, half an AU apart, one bone-dry and one a quarter ice — the snow line preserved in place.

But the one I find most compelling is **the Galilean moons**, because it is not a repetition of the same event but an *independent instance* of the same mechanism at a completely different scale. When Jupiter formed it was hot, wrapped in its own miniature disk with its own temperature gradient — so it should have imposed its own condensation sequence, and its moons should show a compositional gradient with distance. They do: density falls monotonically outward, from bone-dry Io (5,900 kg/m³, the most volcanic body in the solar system, no water at all) through Europa and Ganymede to half-ice Callisto (1,830). **This is the snow line reproduced around a different central body, ten thousand times smaller, by the same physics** — exactly the kind of confirmation that turns a plausible story into an established mechanism.

{{image: Galilean moons | Jupiter's four Galilean moons — Io, Europa, Ganymede, and Callisto — to scale. Their densities fall steadily with distance from Jupiter, from rocky, waterless Io nearest in to half-ice Callisto farthest out, exactly as the planets' densities fall with distance from the Sun. Jupiter formed hot, surrounded by its own miniature disk with its own temperature gradient, and imposed its own condensation sequence on the moons forming around it. The snow line, reproduced at a ten-thousandfold smaller scale — an independent instance of the same physics.}}

Taken together, three independent lines of evidence — planetary densities, asteroid compositions with position, and a miniature repetition around Jupiter — all confirm the same mechanism. The snow line is not a convenient story; it is a physical boundary whose existence, location, and consequences can be checked several independent ways, and which passes each check.

## Does the line hold elsewhere? The exoplanet test

There is one more test, the most severe of all, because it applies the theory to systems it was never designed to describe. The prediction is clear: **giant planets should form beyond the snow line**, because only there is there enough solid material to build a critical core in time. Then, in 1995, the first planet around a Sun-like star was found — **51 Pegasi b**, roughly Jupiter's mass, orbiting at **0.05 AU** in 4.2 days, twenty times closer than Mercury, deep inside where a giant could not possibly form. These "hot Jupiters" looked, at first, like a fatal blow.

The resolution was not to abandon the theory but to add to it: **51 Pegasi b did not form where it was found. It formed beyond the snow line, as the theory says — and then it moved.** A planet embedded in a massive gas disk can exchange angular momentum with it and spiral inward. This is **planetary migration**, and it is the subject of a coming chapter — where it will turn out to apply to our own giants too. The snow-line account of *where giants form* survived intact; what had to be discarded was the tacit assumption that *planets stay where they form.*

With thousands of exoplanets now catalogued, the statistics deliver a split verdict, and both halves matter. A genuine confirmation: giant planets are preferentially found (or inferred to originate) beyond the snow-line distance, and they are substantially more common around **metal-rich stars** — stars whose disks had more solid material, exactly as core accretion requires. But a humbling result too: hot Jupiters orbit only about 1% of stars, and the *most common* planets found are ones our solar system lacks entirely — **super-Earths and mini-Neptunes**, packed close to their stars. Our own architecture, with a conspicuous gap where the galaxy's most common planets should be, is **not the typical arrangement**. So the mechanism generalizes — condensation, core accretion, and the snow line describe how giants form — but our particular outcome does not. **Where a planet forms and where it ends up are two different questions**, and having settled the first, we will soon have to take up the second.

## The line that wrote the solar system

Gather the argument. We began at a boundary you can still see: the asteroid belt's gradient from dry Vesta to wet Ceres, marking where the nebula crossed the freezing point of water. We characterized the disk — a minimum-mass solar nebula of ~4,300 Earth masses, ten to thirty times what survives, 98.5% gas and 1% solids, with water outweighing all rock. We laid out the condensation sequence and saw that because temperature fell with distance, it became a sequence in space — a chemically stratified disk. We located the line by calculation: $T(r) \approx 280\,\text{K} \times r^{-1/2}$ gives $r = (280/170)^2 = 2.71$ AU — while being honest that the line moved inward as the disk cooled. And we found why one line changed everything: it more than doubled the available solids, and *only because water is abundant*, letting outer cores win the race to ten Earth masses before the gas dispersed. Three fossils confirm it — the clean density division, the belt's positional gradient, and the Galilean moons repeating the pattern in miniature.

Here is the four-sentence explanation of the solar system, every clause a physical claim: *Sunlight falls off as the inverse square of distance, so the disk was hot near the Sun and cold far away. Water — the most abundant condensable substance in the universe — could only freeze into solid ice beyond about 2.7 AU, more than doubling the solid material there. Because the disk's gas dispersed within a few million years, only bodies with enough solids to build a ten-Earth-mass core in that time could capture hydrogen envelopes and become giants. So the solar system has four small rocky planets inside that line and four giants outside it.* A description tells you what the solar system looks like; an explanation tells you why it could hardly have looked otherwise. **Next: how microscopic dust grains actually become planets — and the genuine difficulty, the metre-size barrier, that stands in the way.**$astroDisk_master$,
